  "id": "Compound::DB00337 - Disease::DOID:3310",
  "verbalization": "Pimecrolimus, a calcineurin inhibitor, is implicated in the development of eczema through a mechanism involving Tacrolimus, a known activator of the same pathway. The evidence suggests that Pimecrolimus may cause a condition such as eczema herpeticum or herpes simplex dermatitis, both of which are associated with Tacrolimus. Tacrolimus, in turn, is used to treat atopic dermatitis, indicating a potential link between Pimecrolimus and the condition. The shared ancestry with Calcineurin Inhibitors and Macrolides suggests a broader pharmacological context, possibly involving immune modulation. The consistent pathway from Pimecrolimus to Tacrolimus to Atopic Dermatitis highlights a possible mechanism where Pimecrolimus may contribute to the onset of atopic dermatitis through a pathway involving immune dysregulation, though the exact nature of this relationship remains to be fully elucidated.",
